Now analyze duplication: ejectInputs uses items.set(slot, EMPTY) then player.getInventory().add(copy); if add returns false (partially?), player.drop. Vanilla Inventory.add(ItemStack) in 1.21: returns boolean and mutates the stack passed in? Let's recall: Inventory.add(ItemStack stack) calls add(-1, stack) and returns boolean. Actually `public boolean add(ItemStack stack) { return this.add(-1, stack); }` and `boolean add(int slot, ItemStack stack)` ... `this.add(slot, stack)` ... The stack passed is mutated: in Inventory.add(int, ItemStack): if stack.isEmpty() return false; ... if stack.getCount() != i) { ... } In implementation, it does `stack.setCount(...)` — actually the item stack argument is consumed/shrunk. Hmm, but here copy is passed and not reused; if add partially fits, add returns true and remaining? In vanilla Inventory.add returns true if any added? Actually in 1.20.1 Inventory.add: 
```
public boolean add(ItemStack stack) { return this.add(-1, stack); }
public boolean add(int slot, ItemStack stack) {
  if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;
  try {
    if (stack.isDamaged()) { ... } else {
      int i;
      do {
        i = stack.getCount();
        if (slot == -1) { stack.setCount(this.add(-1, stack)); } else { stack.setCount(this.add(slot, stack)); }
      } while (stack.getCount() > 0 && ...)
      ...
    }
  }
}
```
I shouldn't assert vanilla behavior without evidence. Since copy is not read after, if add partially consumes and returns false, player.drop(copy) would drop remaining (mutated copy) — fine. If add returns true on partial and leaves remainder? Then remainder lost—but only copy mutated, and original was cleared. This is a potential unverified vanilla semantic. Must mark as unverified because no decompiled source in workspace. Better: note that ejectInputs counts on player.getInventory().add semantics which are game library behavior not observed; but the pattern `if (!add(copy)) drop(copy)` is standard. The copy is not re-read after add, so any remainder not dropped by add's return-false path would be lost; whether vanilla add can return true with leftover non-empty stack is not verifiable here. That's an unverified candidate, not confirmed bug.

Also fuel slot SLOT_FUEL is NOT ejected in ejectInputs (only FIRST, SECOND, OUTPUT). Is that intended? Docs say "材料与产物都退回" — fuel excluded. Not a defect per se; fuel persists. But progress = 0 while fuel remains; that's consistent (fuel not part of process state). Not item loss.

Duplicate return risks:
1. ejectInputs then block break: eject clears slots, so dropContents only drops remaining fuel. No duplication.
2. Drop contents on block break: AlchemyFurnaceBlock.onRemove calls dropContents → Containers.dropContents(level, pos, this). Also BaseEntityBlock/Block onRemove via player destroy: vanilla `Block.playerDestroy` drops loot table (block item) and `Block.dropResources`. `onRemove` is called after block entity removed? In Level.setBlock, when removing block entity, `Level.setBlock` ... Actually BlockEntity is removed via `Level.removeBlockEntity` which calls `blockEntity.setRemoved()`. onRemove is called during setBlock before block entity removal? In 1.21 Level.setBlock: `this.getChunkAt(pos).setBlockState(pos, state, flags)`; onRemove is called in Level.setBlock after chunk set? The block entity is removed by `onRemove`? Level.setBlock: 
```
BlockState blockstate = chunk.getBlockState(pos);
Block block = state.getBlock();
if (blockstate.is(block)) ... 
...
BlockState blockstate1 = chunk.setBlockState(pos, state, ...);
if (blockstate1 == null) return false;
...
if (blockstate.hasBlockEntity() && (!blockstate.is(state.getBlock()) || !state.hasBlockEntity())) {
    this.removeBlockEntity(pos);
}
...
state.onPlace(...)
if (blockstate.is(block)) ... blockstate.onRemove(this, pos, state, flag)
```
Hmm order: in 1.21 `Level.setBlock`:
```
if (blockstate.hasBlockEntity() && (!blockstate.is(block) || !state.hasBlockEntity())) {
    this.removeBlockEntity(pos);
}
```
This removes the block entity BEFORE onRemove is called. If so, AlchemyFurnaceBlock.onRemove's `level.getBlockEntity(pos)` returns null and dropContents never runs → items lost when breaking furnace! That's a very plausible real bug and key finding. But I need evidence. Is Minecraft source available in workspace? Check for decompiled sources / .gradle caches maybe. Let me check repo for dependencies. We can look for a sources jar in gradle caches. Use glob for Level.java? C:/Users/.../.gradle maybe not in workspace. Tools may be restricted to workspace. Let's check.

Also note `state.is(newState.getBlock())` condition: for block break, newState is air, so condition true if getBlockEntity returns. Need Evidence whether BE exists at that time. In NeoForge 1.21, Level.setBlock order I recall:
```
BlockState blockstate = chunk.getBlockState(pos);
Block block = state.getBlock();
if (blockstate.is(block)) { ... }
...
BlockState blockstate1 = chunk.setBlockState(pos, state, (flags & 64) != 0);
if (blockstate1 == null) return false;
...
if (blockstate.hasBlockEntity() && (!blockstate.is(block) || !state.hasBlockEntity())) {
    this.removeBlockEntity(pos);
}
```
Wait actually I recall in 1.20 `Level.setBlock`:
```
BlockState blockstate = chunk.getBlockState(pos);
Block block = state.getBlock();
if (blockstate.is(block)) { ... return false }
...
BlockState blockstate1 = chunk.setBlockState(pos, state, ...);
if (blockstate1 == null) return false;
...
if (blockstate.hasBlockEntity() && (!blockstate.is(block) || !state.hasBlockEntity())) {
   this.removeBlockEntity(pos);
}
```
Hmm, but onRemove is called by ChunkAccess? Actually `LevelChunk.setBlockState` calls `blockState.onRemove(this.level, pos, state, movedByPiston)`? I think in 1.21 `LevelChunk.setBlockState`:
```
BlockState blockstate = section.setBlockState(...);
...
if (blockstate != null) {
   ...
   blockstate.onRemove(this.level, pos, state, flag);
   ...
}
```
So onRemove might be called inside chunk.setBlockState, before Level.removeBlockEntity. Then getBlockEntity works. That matters. Without source in workspace, mark unverified, and inspect whether other blocks in the mod use this same pattern (SharedCacheBlock does direct Containers.dropContents too, presumably tested/working). Presence of same pattern elsewhere suggests it works. Good enough for "no confirmed defect" but note unverified engine ordering.

Let me check whether Minecraft sources are available in workspace (unlikely). Also check tests maybe a LogicCheck asserting eject semantics. Search "eject" in test found none. Also check docs/08 acceptance criteria re furnace.

Let me check for a button in screen: none. So conclusion: 退料按钮在 AlchemyFurnaceScreen 中不存在；id==1 只在 clickMenuButton 定义里，客户端没有 sendButton 调用。This means the "player eject button" entry is not reachable through mod UI — feature incomplete, but not item loss. Actually maybe some other payload? grep "clickMenuButton" only definition. So yes.

Another potential: clickMenuButton id==0 returns true even if player... fine.

Duplicate: In clickMenuButton, `furnace.stillValid(player)` checks distance; container is BE. Server-side validation fine. No client trust.

Close menu: removed() → super.removed then container.stopOpen. Menu closes without returning items; items stay in BE, progress continues in serverTick even when menu closed. Not item duplication/loss. But careful: AlchemyFurnaceMenu client-side constructor uses `new SimpleContainer(4)` and SimpleContainerData(6) — client screen reads recipe preview and status from data slots. Fine.

But there's a subtle bug: the client-side menu's container is SimpleContainer, so clickMenuButton on client returns false at instanceof check — but client doesn't call it. Not relevant.

Potential state residue: eject sets progress=0 but does not reset status or fuel or lastCatchup*. status gets recomputed next tick anyway. lastCatchupSeconds/items remain showing old "catchup" info in UI (AlchemyFurnaceScreen lines 123-127) — prior settlement counters not cleared by eject. That's "工序状态残留" cosmetic: progress reset to 0, status remains stale until tick. Also ejectInputs doesn't reset status — but tickOnce recomputes. Not a functional defect.

More significant: ejectInputs returns fuel not ejected → fuel remains, intended.

Another: In ejectInputs, progress reset to 0 even though items returned; if user had partial progress, consumed fuel for progress but no output: fuel consumed and progress lost — that's intended ejection cost? Arguably fuel loss is a design consequence, not item duplication. Note as observation: 退料不退燃料槽，且已耗燃料不返还，progress 归零；这是有意的？docs say materials+products returned, so fuel not included. Fine.

Potential duplication: none; `items.set(slot, EMPTY)` before add/drop prevents copy staying in furnace. If player is null? Not possible ServerPlayer.

Potential item loss in dropContents: Containers.dropContents drops all items including... it iterates container.getContainerSize. Fine. But block break also triggers onRemove for chunk unload? onRemove is not called on chunk unload (that's onChunkUnload/ setRemoved). Actually onRemove is called when block is replaced/removed, but also when chunk unloads? No.

Important: `onRemove` condition uses `!state.is(newState.getBlock())`; for block break newState=air → true; for replacing with same block → false; for Chunk unload there is no setBlock. However, is dropContents called twice if a block is removed via explosion? `Block.playerDestroy` + `onRemove` — onRemove drops contents once. OK.

But duplication risk: `onRemove` is called in various paths e.g., block state changed by other mods? drops once. Also `onRemove` could run on both client and server? Client level also calls onRemove? If so dropContents on client spawns client-side item entities, harmless. Actually client `level.getBlockEntity` exists; Containers.dropContents adds item entities client-side → visual ghosts. But block breaking is client-side predicted too; onRemove is called on client when block removed via setBlock? Client normally receives block update and sets block, triggering onRemove on client. That would spawn client-side item entities duplicating visuals. Hmm, but many vanilla blocks guard with level.isClientSide? Vanilla e.g. BaseContainerBlockEntity drops contents in `playerDestroy` (Block.playerDestroy) which is server-only. Here onRemove lacks isClientSide check, so client may spawn phantom drops. Does Level.setBlock on client call onRemove? Yes, onRemove is called from ChunkAccess/LevelChunk setBlockState on both sides. So client-side phantom item entities likely. Vanilla `ChestBlock` uses `onRemove`? ShulkerBoxBlock overrides `playerDestroy`; `AbstractFurnaceBlock`? Furnace drops contents in `playerDestroy` and `onRemove`? In 1.20 AbstractFurnaceBlock has `onRemove`? Actually AbstractFurnaceBlock overrides `playerWillDestroy` (server check) to drop contents. Dispenser/Dropper override `onRemove` and use `Containers.dropContents` but with `level.isClientSide`? Let me recall: `DispenserBlock` has `onRemove` calling `Containers.dropContents`? I think in 1.20 `DispenserBlock`:
```
@Override
public void onRemove(BlockState state, Level level, BlockPos pos, BlockState newState, boolean isMoving) {
   if (!state.is(newState.getBlock())) {
      BlockEntity blockentity = level.getBlockEntity(pos);
      if (blockentity instanceof DispenserBlockEntity) {
         Containers.dropContents(level, pos, (DispenserBlockEntity)blockentity);
         level.updateNeighbourForOutputSignal(pos, this);
      }
      super.onRemove(...);
   }
}
```
No isClientSide guard. Since onRemove client-side would create client items... perhaps client doesn't call onRemove in setBlock? But SharedCacheBlock uses same pattern, and NeoForge/vanilla examples exist. I won't claim this without source. Mark unverified. Also note client phantom items cannot be picked up into server inventory (client entity is separate), so no real dupe — but "未观察" .

Actually I could check if MC sources are present in project (.gradle caches under user home). Tools resolve relative to workspace; maybe path "C:/Users/杨铭/Desktop/修仙模组/.gradle" contains? Let's glob for Level.java maybe outside via path param. Try glob path "C:/Users/杨铭/.gradle/caches" pattern "**/Level.java" — expensive and maybe out of scope. The instruction says only mod-own paths; parent class behavior unverified. Good.

Now: also check whether `player.inventoryMenu.broadcastChanges()` is right to sync the furnace menu — it broadcasts the player's inventory menu, not the open furnace menu. But BE setChanged + container sync (AbstractContainerMenu broadcastChanges via block entity? serverTick... AbstractContainerMenu.sendAllDataToRemote) — the furnace menu slots sync through `ServerPlayer.tick` → `containerMenu.broadcastChanges()` each tick for open menu. So clearing items in BE while menu open: `items.set` doesn't call setChanged per slot but setChanged called; slot sync happens because menu broadcasts comparing slots to remote. Since menu's slots read live container, broadcastChanges will send changes. `player.inventoryMenu.broadcastChanges()` syncs the player's own inventory menu not the furnace menu — likely unnecessary/wrong target, but furnace menu sync occurs next tick anyway. Note as observation (not item loss). Entity inventoryMenu is always "open" for the player.

Potential real duplication: none identified in mod code.

Potential loss: `finishRecipe` grows output but tickOnce only calls finishRecipe if canOutput already checked earlier in same tick — canOutput requires output count < max stack. If output has count 63 and recipe output 1+mode bonus 2 → grow to 65 > 64? canOutput check at line 165/176 only ensures output.getCount() < maxStackSize (63 < 64 true), then produced count may be 2 (balanced mode output count 1... mode 2 adds 1 → 2; mode 1 adds 1 → 2 unless residue). If output 63 and produced 2 → 65 > max stack. ItemStack.grow beyond max is allowed and count 65 can exist; may cause weird behavior/loss on save? That's a real edge: overstack. Also output count can exceed max stack. Is it item loss/dup? Not exactly; could cause client desync or item loss when inserted into inventory. It's a mod-source-confirmed overflow of stack beyond max (mode 2 always +1). Hmm, `canOutput` should check `output.getCount() + produced <= max`. But produced computed later including mode. This is a genuine source-confirmable defect: 产物槽可能超过 64。But task asks about item loss/duplication/state residue; overstack is adjacent. I can report as source-confirmed finding but label as not item-eaten? Let's verify canOutput semantics precisely: output.getCount() < output.getMaxStackSize() → allows 63 with max 64. finishRecipe produced count = outputCount + 1 (mode 2) = 2, grow → 65. So yes possible. This is source-reasoned, though whether MC handles count 65 gracefully unclear (ItemStack allows any count via grow/setCount; saving/loading count >99? fine up to). Might cause client slot rendering >64 but not item loss necessarily. Report as "源码可见的边界缺陷（产物槽可超过最大堆叠）", note consequences unverified.

Also another duplication-ish: in AlchemyFurnaceBlock.onRemove, condition `!state.is(newState.getBlock())`; if block replaced by another alchemy furnace? same block → false, no drop, but block entity recreated and items lost? Setting same block state with setBlockAndUpdate same block: blockstate.is(block) true so setBlock returns false; no removal. Not issue.

Double-drop risk: Block break: does vanilla drop container contents too? No.

Now check whether `dropContents` can run twice: In `onRemove`, super.onRemove. Fine. Also player breaking with shift? no.

Eject + break race: single-threaded server; fine.

Now confirm client UI button absent. Let me grep "addRenderableWidget" and "Button" in AlchemyFurnaceScreen — read file, none. Also no key binding for furnace eject. Also no network payload sending button id. So entry "玩家退料按钮" is unimplemented at client. But maybe vanilla's menu button can be triggered only via packet; players cannot. So feature claim in docs not reachable.

Wait — check docs mention "客户端与服务端共用同一套菜单校验" and "退料按钮" implemented? Perhaps there's a screen elsewhere (e.g., AlchemyFurnaceScreen may add a Button in a newer version)? We read current file: 129 lines, no button. Confirmed absent.

Let me also check git history? no need.

Also check tests for furnace logic in LogicCheck — maybe there's an `alchemy` section asserting eject. grep "furnace" in test.

